<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xenophanes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570–c. 478 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Xenophanes of Colophon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xenophanes of Colophon was a Greek poet and philosopher who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> after the Persian conquest and spent a long life wandering the Greek world, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In surviving fragments of his verse he mocked the human-like gods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> and argued for a single, unmoving deity. He also reflected on the limits of human knowledge and on fossils as evidence of past seas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophanes.jpg" alt="Xenophanes" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The ancient biographer Diogenes Laertius reports that Xenophanes was born in Colophon, a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and says he flourished in the 60th Olympiad (540–537 BCE). Modern scholars generally place his birth around 570–560 BCE. His surviving work refers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epimenides">Epimenides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>, and he is himself mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclitus">Heraclitus</a> and Epicharmus.</p><p>By his own account he was an itinerant poet who left his native land at the age of 25 and lived 67 years in other Greek lands, dying at or after the age of 92. Ancient testimony says he buried his sons, but little else about him or his family can be reliably established. His physical theories were probably influenced by the Milesians; his view that the rainbow is clouds may be read as a response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximenes">Anaximenes</a>, who held that it is light reflected off clouds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Xenophanes wrote a variety of poems, and his views are known only from fragments quoted by later writers. Diogenes Laertius says he wrote a poem of about 2,000 lines on the foundation of Colophon and Elea, and later testimony suggests his satires, called Silloi, filled at least five books. The title On Nature was probably given by librarians of the ancient world, and his remarks on natural philosophy were likely made incidentally in his satires. He composed elegiac couplets criticising traditional values of wealth, excess and athletic victory, and held that cultivating wisdom mattered more.</p><p>He criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> for representing the gods as foolish or morally weak, and held that God is one, supreme among gods and men, and unlike mortals in body or mind. He sought natural explanations for clouds, rainbows and St. Elmo's fire. From fossils of sea creatures found above land he concluded that water had once covered the earth. He distinguished knowledge from belief, holding that an objective truth exists but that mortals cannot know it, and the sceptic Sextus Empiricus quotes his verses on the point.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later ancient writers associated him with Elea, as founder of the Eleatic school and teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-elea">Zeno of Elea</a>. Modern scholars generally find little justification for this, and note that no ancient writer expressly says he ever was at Elea. Timon of Phlius praised him as nearly a perfect sceptic, and the ancient tradition saw him as the first in a line leading to Pyrrhonism. He has been called the founder of epistemology, the first sceptic, an empiricist and a reformer of religion, while Karl Popper read him as a precursor of critical rationalism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Metaphysics summarised his view as the All is God.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xenophanes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philosophers questioned the traditional gods: in the 6th century BCE <b>Xenophanes</b> mocked the idea of gods in human form, and in 399 BCE Socrates was condemned to death in Athens for impiety.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Allegorical readings were popular and were used to defend Homer against charges, made by the philosopher <b>Xenophanes</b>, that he disrespected the gods; Porphyry read the cave of the nymphs as an image of human life.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The elegiac poet Mimnermus and the philosopher <b>Xenophanes</b> were Colophonians.</blockquote><a href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Colophon, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.12,27.14&amp;year=-511" aria-label="Colophon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Xenophanes"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4808 4306h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4808 4306h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.12,27.14&amp;year=-511">What was here in 511 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Homer and Hesiod have ascribed to the gods all things that are a shame and a disgrace among mortals, stealings and adulteries and deceivings of one another.&#8221;</p><footer>Fragments (Burnet 11) &#183; tr. John Burnet, 1908 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67097/pg67097-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes, and if oxen and horses or lions had hands, and could paint with their hands, and produce works of art as men do, horses would paint the forms of the gods like horses, and oxen like oxen…&#8221;</p><footer>Fragments (Burnet 15) &#183; tr. John Burnet, 1908 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67097/pg67097-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The gods have not revealed all things to men from the beginning, but by seeking they find in time what is better.&#8221;</p><footer>Fragments (Burnet 16) &#183; tr. John Burnet, 1908 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67097/pg67097-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
